#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068481 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068713 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e122a1fe-f340-5af6-9908-68ad52f89731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form"}] :question/id #uuid "5cf6d36a-0e67-5f16-befd-32d30cf4b930" :question/math-academy-id "e-14010" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=5x+3$ and $y=x-1$ sketched below.\n\n![](images/e6/e63b2ac5fe2bdfb905fe3c86706be4a556e5322698f9fd26ee115dba0a886f4b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations\n\n$$\ny=5x+3\n$$\n\nand $y=x-1$. So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n{y = 5x + 3; y = x - 1\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Substituting the first equation\n\n$$\ny=5x+3\n$$\n\ninto the second equation $y=x-1$ gives an equation that we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 3 &= x - 1 \\\\\n5x &= x - 4 \\\\\n4x &= -4 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=x-1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x - 1 \\\\\n&=-1 - 1 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is\n\n$$\nx=-1, \\, y=-2\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(-1,-2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068714 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a81c1cd-7381-5788-8a51-53cecee5f600" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "7fe8c6a0-f152-5349-9152-b4c018368dbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14012" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $x + 2 y = 1$ and $2 x - y = 7$." :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n{x + 2y = 1; 2x - y = 7\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{x + 2y = 1; 2x - y = 7 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 2\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n4x - 2y &= 14 \\\\\n(x + 4x) &= (1 + 14) \\\\\n5x &= 15 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=3$ into the equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=1\n$$\n\nto solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n3 + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n2y &= -2 \\\\\ny &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is\n\n$$\nx=3, \\, y=-1\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(3,-1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068483 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b154eb5d-a45b-5d92-9e33-dd7875327e6e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Unknown Parameters Given a Point of Intersection"}] :question/id #uuid "cceef15c-a242-5486-a830-f6bdf9f07010" :question/math-academy-id "e-14015" :question/problem "The lines $y = -3 x + a$ and $y = x - 1$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(b, -4)$. Find the parameters $a$ and $b$." :question/worked-solution "Since $(b,-4)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values\n\n$$\nx=b, \\, y=-4\n$$\n\nmust solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{y =-3x + a; y = x - 1\n$$\n\nWe substitute\n\n$$\nx=b, \\, y=-4\n$$\n\nto obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n{-4 =-3b + a; - 4 = b - 1\n$$\n\nIn the second equation, we can solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4 &= b - 1 \\\\\n- 3 &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $b=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4 &= -3b + a \\\\\n- 4 &= -3(-3) + a \\\\\n- 4 &= 9 + a \\\\\n- 13 &= a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = -13$ and $b = -3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068713 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e122a1fe-f340-5af6-9908-68ad52f89731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "328893f1-1bfe-5d8b-b29a-5733b229fb9a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066105} {:answer-field/id #uuid "3c1bfb5b-8661-579d-bbb2-a888c6e3cec3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066106}] :question/id #uuid "e5474d40-cfd8-5d52-b2b0-e62e8f7d76fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-208830" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $y = 3x - 1$ and $y =-4x + 6$ has coordinates$($\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068714 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a81c1cd-7381-5788-8a51-53cecee5f600" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Lines Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "87a569e7-b9ff-5249-82a9-9efcf843d4cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066130} {:answer-field/id #uuid "3e1b4ecb-e59f-573e-9fd5-712b1fdac694" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066131}] :question/id #uuid "ce310a81-63ea-5437-b6a7-508ae82b0078" :question/math-academy-id "q-215423" :question/problem "The point of intersection of the lines $x - y = 7$ and $3x + 2y = 11$ has coordinates$($\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."}]}
